  "visual": {
    "visualType": "textbox",
    "objects": {
      "general": [
        {
          "properties": {
            "paragraphs": [
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "7 \u00b7 Terminal Value \u2014 derived from scratch",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "13pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "After year 5 the business keeps producing cash forever, growing at g. Sum that infinite stream at rate r:",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "TV = CF\u2086/(1+r) + CF\u2086(1+g)/(1+r)\u00b2 + CF\u2086(1+g)\u00b2/(1+r)\u00b3 + \u2026  Each term is the last one \u00d7 (1+g)/(1+r) \u2014 a geometric series with ratio < 1 (because r > g), so it SUMS to a finite number:",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "TV = CF\u2086 \u00f7 (r \u2212 g),  and CF\u2086 = FCF\u2085 \u00d7 (1+g).  So:  TV = FCF\u2085 \u00d7 (1+g) \u00f7 (WACC \u2212 g) = 235.1 \u00d7 1.025 \u00f7 (0.0911 \u2212 0.025) = 241.0 \u00f7 0.0661 = 3,646 ($3.65B).",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "That TV sits at the END of year 5, so discount it home: PV(TV) = 3,646 \u00f7 1.0911^5 = 3,646 \u00f7 1.546 = 2,358 ($2.36B). If r \u2264 g the series never converges \u2014 a perpetuity growing faster than its discount rate is infinite, which is why the model refuses it.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "\u2192 tab 6, section 2",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "textRuns": [
                  {
                    "value": "Where g comes from: it is an ASSUMPTION (2.5%), not a computed number \u2014 no statement or market series can tell you a company's forever-growth rate. Discipline: anchor it near long-run inflation (~2\u20132.5%), never above long-run GDP growth (~4\u20135%, or the company eventually IS the economy), and always below WACC (the convergence condition above). It lives in the export's explicit-assumptions block, flows in as terminal_growth_pct, and the sensitivity grid runs it 1.5\u20133.5% precisely because it is a judgment call.",
                    "textStyle": {
                      "fontSize": "9pt"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    "drillFilterOtherVisuals": true
  }
}
